// LoxOps.Stringify (runtime/clr/src/LoxOps.cs) recurses once per level of
// list nesting, with no depth guard at all — unbounded by LoxClosure's own
// FramesMax, because it is plain C# recursion over a value's own shape,
// not a Lox-level call. Printing a list nested this many levels deep
// overflows the CLR's default 8 MiB thread stack partway through
// formatting the resulting StackOverflowException's own message, which
// CoreCLR can then only report as a bare process abort — no Lox.LoxError
// line, no stdout at all (measured with tools/clr_run.sh's LoxHost
// indirection bypassed, running the emitted assembly directly). LoxHost's
// larger stack (tools/clr_run.sh) clears this depth with a wide margin.
//
// This depth also has to stay under native's OWN process-stack ceiling:
// check_clr_probes.sh runs native first and fails this probe by name if
// that run alone fails, and src/object.cpp's stringifyObj has the
// identical unguarded-recursion shape (issue #152). Measured on the
// default 8 MiB process stack, native holds through 25,000 levels and
// segfaults by 27,000, so this file's 20,000 carries about a 1.3x margin
// under native's own ceiling, not a wide one, and that ceiling moves with
// whatever process stack limit the script runs under. Both gates that run
// this probe — check_clr_probes.sh's own run_native, and
// tools/diff_runtimes.py's _raise_native_stack_limit — raise this floor for
// NATIVE's own child process only, never for the CLR side. A host whose
// HARD limit is already capped below that floor is the one case neither
// raise can cover. The CLR side is left at the caller's ambient default on
// purpose: this probe's whole point is proving LoxHost's own larger thread
// (runtime/clr/host/LoxHost.cs), not an ambient limit raised for every
// child of the gate script, is what clears this depth — a regression that
// stops routing a CLR run through LoxHost still fails this probe, because
// nothing here gives the resulting `dotnet` process a bigger stack than
// whatever the host already had.
//
// Lives outside the parent translation-probes directory because the JVM
// gates — the curated arrays in tools/check_jvm_probes.sh, and the CI
// step's non-recursive walk of notes/translation-probes/ against the JVM
// backend — never name a file one level under clr-only/; only the CI
// walk would otherwise pick this file up if it lived one level higher,
// and the JVM backend already matches native at this same depth
// (tools/jvm_run.sh's own -Xss64m default clears it with room to spare),
// so moving it up would add nothing past what that one walk already
// covers. This is the opposite of why 31_deep_recursion.lox lives here:
// that probe is moved out because the JVM backend DIVERGES from native
// (no frame-count ceiling of its own); this one is moved out because the
// JVM backend already MATCHES native. check_clr_probes.sh names this
// file directly, so the CLR checkpoint still runs it.
var x = 0;
var n = 0;
while (n < 20000) {
    x = [x];
    n = n + 1;
}
print x;
